From d1a06ab6d11d280c0b47f09d48f9ac4a42ca8094 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zsolt Mencseli <4507993+zsoltmencseli@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:40:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Canvas DnD: take over only over canvas windows, keep
 Hyprland's drag motion

Review follow-up for #19. The first version reset Hyprland's drag-motion
listener on the first canvas motion, so for the rest of the drag targets
that Hyprland focuses itself (a fullscreen window on another monitor, a
panel on the top layer) got enter but no motion.

Now the canvas only drives a drag while the pointer is over a window it
draws, on its own monitor (CanvasDndRoute.hpp):
- canvas window: the canvas picks the target and sends surface-local motion;
- top/overlay layer surface under the pointer: Hyprland (panels win over
  canvas windows at the same point);
- another monitor, or a monitor whose canvas stepped aside for a fullscreen
  window: Hyprland;
- empty canvas: no target, so a drop there cancels;
- after the drop, until the target finishes: the canvas holds still.

Hyprland's drag-motion listener stays installed for the whole drag. It is
held back only while the drag target is the canvas-picked window (for that
surface it measures from the real position); when Hyprland moves the target
elsewhere its motion runs unchanged. The hold-back is per drag, forgotten
when the drag ends, and undone on plugin unload.

Buttons are never swallowed during a drag (the release is the drop,
wherever it lands), and Escape is left to Hyprland so it can cancel. The
camera/focus-follow guards for windows opening mid-drag stay.

Tests: tests/canvas-dnd-route-test.cpp (make test-dnd-route) checks the
routing and replays the review's cases against a model of Hyprland's drag
handling; tests/dnd-nested.py drives a real drag in a nested two-monitor
session (canvas window, top-layer panel, fullscreen window on the other
monitor, drop on empty canvas, repeat, source killed mid-drag).
